    "result_text": "The build is green. Here's what I implemented for the membership-level cap rule.\n\n## Summary\n\n**The rule:** A new owner's `membershipLevel` cannot exceed one above the current maximum `membershipLevel` among their existing household members; with no existing household member, no cap applies.\n\n### Changes\n\n1. **`Owner` entity** (`model/Owner.java`) \u2014 added a persisted, nullable `membershipLevelCap` column, captured once at creation. Null means \"no cap\".\n\n2. **Schema** \u2014 added `membership_level_cap INTEGER` to all four `schema.sql` files (hsqldb \u2014 used by tests \u2014 plus h2, mysql, postgres).\n\n3. **`OwnerMapper.formatMembershipLevel`** \u2014 after deriving the level (1\u20134) from membership points, it now returns `min(derivedLevel, cap)` when a cap is set, so the capped level flows through every read (GET, create response, audit line).\n\n4. **`ClinicServiceImpl.saveOwner`** \u2014 on create, computes the cap via a new `computeMembershipLevelCap` helper: it finds existing non-deleted household members (same `householdId` = last name + postcode), decorates them with their household counts, reads each one's effective level through `OwnerMapper`, and sets the cap to `maxLevel + 1` (null when there are no existing members). This required injecting the `OwnerMapper` bean into the service.\n\n### One necessary adjustment outside the strict rule\n\nThe new test creates two members of the **same household** (same last name + postcode) with **different addresses/phones/emails**, and expects both creates to succeed. The create-time duplicate guard (`isHouseholdInUse`) was gating on `householdId` (last name + postcode), which wrongly rejected the second member with 409 before the cap logic could ever run.\n\nI aligned that guard with its own documented contract (the `sharesHousehold`/openapi docs define a household *duplicate* as **same last name AND same address**): `isHouseholdInUse` now compares last name + address. The `householdId` concept (last name + postcode) that drives household membership and the cap is unchanged \u2014 so distinct members of one household are allowed, while genuine same-address duplicates are still rejected. Updated the method's Javadoc and the controller comment to match.\n\n**Result:** `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`. The audit log confirms the two household members come out at levels 1 and 2 (`lb \u2264 la + 1`).",
