    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change slots into the existing conventions cleanly:\n\n- **Right unit for the cap computation.** `computeMembershipLevelCap` is a private helper on `ClinicServiceImpl` called from the `owner.isNew()` block of `saveOwner`, directly alongside its siblings `findPossibleDuplicateOf`, `countNamesakes`, and `generateCustomerCode`. It follows the same shape (guard on null household id, derive-once-at-creation, store on the entity). A maintainer would expect exactly this placement.\n\n- **Right unit for applying the cap.** The ceiling is applied inside `OwnerMapper.formatMembershipLevel`, which is already the single home for \"effective membership level\" derivation. Keeping the `min(derived, cap)` there \u2014 rather than capping-and-storing the level \u2014 preserves the existing dynamic derivation while pinning only the ceiling, and it's thoroughly documented. All level logic stays in one place.\n\n- **Good reuse, no meaningful duplication.** It reuses `decorateWithHouseholdMemberCount(members, members)` (the same two-arg form used by the list/page paths) and `ownerMapper.formatMembershipLevel` instead of re-deriving levels, so each member's max reflects its own already-capped level. Injecting `OwnerMapper` into the service is a clean one-directional dependency (the mapper depends on nothing), and it's the lesser evil versus duplicating the derivation.\n\n- **Supporting changes are coherent.** The `isHouseholdInUse` rewrite, its interface javadoc, and the controller comment all move together to make \"distinct household members\" (same last name/postcode, different address) legal \u2014 the precondition that makes a per-household cap meaningful \u2014 and the doc updates (Owner field, OpenAPI, schema.sql across all four dialects) stay consistent.\n\nThe inline `findAll().stream()...household-id filter` predicate now appears in both `findPossibleDuplicateOf` and `computeMembershipLevelCap`, but every household/namesake helper in 